data amortization_months_unit_note: String = "Count of calendar billing months for setup-fee amortization — a named Count carrier, not SI duration (cf. billing_month_as_hour_count for the hourly divisor convention only)."

type ClockBasis
  = CpuClock
  | WallClock

data measure_clock_basis_note: String = "WHICH CLOCK a duration was read from, carried as data rather than spelled in a field name (operator ruling 2026-08-05). The defect this closes is not recording one clock instead of two — recording BOTH is correct — it is a duration whose meaning lives only in the name of the field holding it. A figure called wall_nanos and a figure called cpu_nanos are two magnitudes that compare, add and threshold as if they were the same quantity, and nothing in the type stops it: the fast-lane cap is enforced on CPU while every cost receipt recorded wall, and four sessions spent an evening comparing 1005 against 5004, 2507 against 5003 and 7944 against 5018 -- every number correct, only the bases differing, and no test could have caught any of it. THE REPO ALREADY RULED ON THIS SHAPE one magnitude over: extdeps.pricing.object_storage byte_basis_note opens with 'the unit word is not the unit' and resolves vendor GB-vs-GiB ambiguity with a typed ByteBasis on the row, never a second field named gibibytes beside one named gigabytes. This is that resolution for clocks. TWO ARMS AND NOT THREE, deliberately, and this is where it DIVERGES from ByteBasis: ByteBasisUnstated exists because vendors publish rates without saying which byte they mean, so the ambiguity is an external fact we can only record. A clock basis is never external -- this repository takes its own measurements and always knows which clock it read -- so an Unstated arm would model an absence that cannot occur here, and an uninhabited arm is its own defect. Failing to READ a clock is a different fact and is carried by Measured on the value, not by a third basis."

fn clock_basis_eq(a: ClockBasis, b: ClockBasis) -> Bool {
  match a {
    CpuClock =>
      match b {
        CpuClock => true
        WallClock => false
      }
    WallClock =>
      match b {
        CpuClock => false
        WallClock => true
      }
  }
}

fn clock_basis_cpu() -> ClockBasis {
  CpuClock
}

fn clock_basis_wall() -> ClockBasis {
  WallClock
}

data clock_basis_authority_reachability_note: String = "WHY THIS CARRIER LIVES IN std.measure AND NOT WHERE IT WAS FIRST DECLARED, generalized because the same authority hit the same wall twice in one day (2026-08-05). ClockBasis was minted in std.observation, the module whose events first needed it. Within hours it failed to be an authority twice, for two different reasons that look unrelated and are not. First it was internal, and gunbc's migration-policy carrier could not state its own basis without it being EXPORTED. Then std.realization_schedule -- which carried a forked second spelling, WitnessCostClock, that this lane was dissolving -- turned out to be UPSTREAM of std.observation through std.effect_grant and std.effects, so importing the authority created a cycle and the compiler refused it outright. A single authority that half the corpus cannot import is not a single authority. REACHABILITY IS PART OF WHAT MAKES SOMETHING AN AUTHORITY, not a deployment detail discovered afterwards, and it is the part nobody checks when minting one: declaring a concept once is assumed to settle it, when what actually settles it is every consumer being able to name it. The home is therefore chosen by who must reach it, not by who first needed it -- and here that answer is also the honest one on the merits, since which clock a duration was read from is a fact about the MEASUREMENT rather than about the observation event that happens to carry one, this module already owns the magnitudes the basis qualifies, and extdeps.pricing.object_storage ByteBasis is in-repo precedent for a basis living beside its magnitude. A METHOD NOTE, because the cycle was nearly missed: it was checked for by grepping the two files' direct imports, which showed no edge. That is a LOCAL instrument answering a question that is only decidable GLOBALLY, and it returned a confident wrong answer. Only the compiler could see it. When the question is transitive, only a transitive instrument answers it."

data measure_clock_basis_seed_entry_note: String = "Two nullary constructors exist so the v1 seed can hand a ClockBasis across the interpreter boundary WITHOUT building a Value in Rust — the same discipline millisecond(count:) and nanosecond(count:) already carry at this seam, and the reason gunbc.witness_row_cost's seed notes give for it: a carrier assembled on the Rust side is a second copy of this module's shape, and it drifts silently. WHICH one the seed calls is chosen by matching its own typed BudgetKind, so the clock is never recovered from a label string; the function name is a symbol the seed selects, not prose it parses. They dissolve with the seed transport itself, on the witness-realization lane's trigger — when a realized runner enforces the deadline, it constructs the outcome here directly and has nothing to marshal."

fn clock_basis_label(b: ClockBasis) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match b {
    CpuClock => "thread-CPU" as NonEmptyStr
    WallClock => "wall" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

// Derived from the cited ISO 80000-3 factors, not from the linear Scale fold. These exist so the
// squared/cubed relation has a modeled consumer: without them the carriers above would name an
// authority nothing reaches.
//
// BOTH SIDES OF THE CONVERSION ARE TYPED. An earlier revision took a bare Nat, so the one boundary
// where a unit is being CHANGED - the single place a wrong unit is most likely to enter - was the
// one place the substrate accepted an undimensioned number.
fn square_metres_to_square_millimetres(area: SquareMeter) -> SquareMillimeter {
  square_millimeter(count: square_meter_count(m: area) * square_millimetres_per_square_metre())
}

fn cubic_metres_to_cubic_millimetres(volume: CubicMeter) -> CubicMillimeter {
  cubic_millimeter(count: cubic_meter_count(m: volume) * cubic_millimetres_per_cubic_metre())
}

// One full turn in arcseconds, derived from the cited subdivision chain rather than restated.
fn full_turn_arcseconds() -> Arcsecond {
  arcsecond(count: arcseconds_per_turn())
}

fn degrees_to_arcseconds(degrees: Degree) -> Arcsecond {
  arcsecond(count: degree_count(d: degrees) * arcseconds_per_degree_derived())
}

fn turns_to_arcseconds(turns: Turn) -> Arcsecond {
  arcsecond(count: turn_count(t: turns) * arcseconds_per_turn())
}

// A quarter turn is exact in arcseconds because the subdivision is rational - the same reason the
// turn is the canonical unit and radian conversion is absent.
fn quarter_turn_arcseconds() -> Arcsecond {
  arcsecond(count: arcseconds_per_turn() / 4)
}

fn turn(count: std.nat.Nat) -> Turn {
  Measure { count: count }
}

fn turn_count(t: Turn) -> std.nat.Nat {
  measure_count(t)
}

fn signed_square_millimeter(count: Int) -> SignedSquareMillimeter {
  Measure { count: count }
}

fn signed_square_millimeter_count(m: SignedSquareMillimeter) -> Int {
  measure_count(m)
}

data amortization_months_dissolve_on: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(description: "dissolve-on: ground as calendar-month Duration sibling to billing_month_as_hour_count (one month-count authority for billing amortization + hourly divisor), or fold into extdeps.forex/pricing month facts when a second consumer appears — do not mint a third month wrapper.")

data basis_point_dissolve_on: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(description: "dissolve-on: Ratio<Scale> carrier unifying Percent and BasisPoint as two scales of one Dimensionless authority, with bp_to_percent/percent_to_bp derive relations enforced at call sites — else a third dimensionless-ratio use-case mints a third nickname.")

// WHICH ORIGIN a reading was taken against, and it is a DIFFERENT axis from ClockBasis rather than a
// finer spelling of it. ClockBasis says which KIND of clock produced a duration (cpu or wall);
// ClockDomain says which ORIGIN two instants share, and therefore whether subtracting them means
// anything. Both are needed and neither implies the other: two wall readings from independently
// started monotonic clocks are the same basis and still not comparable.
//
// This axis exists because the floor's records cross processes. A coordinator and its worker each
// hold their own monotonic origin, so a Rust `Instant` from one is not orderable against an
// `Instant` from the other — the type permits the subtraction and the answer is meaningless. A
// console timestamp is not a substitute either: it is formatted wall time, reordered by buffering
// and rounded to the millisecond.
type ClockDomain
  = ProcessMonotonic { process: NonEmptyStr }
  | SharedMonotonic { origin: NonEmptyStr }

fn clock_domain_eq(a: ClockDomain, b: ClockDomain) -> Bool {
  match a {
    ProcessMonotonic { process: p } =>
      match b {
        ProcessMonotonic { process: q } => (p as String) == (q as String)
        SharedMonotonic { origin: _ } => false
      }
    SharedMonotonic { origin: o } =>
      match b {
        ProcessMonotonic { process: _ } => false
        SharedMonotonic { origin: p } => (o as String) == (p as String)
      }
  }
}

type ObservationInstant {
  domain: ClockDomain
  basis: ClockBasis
  since_origin: Nanosecond
}

// Ordering is only defined WITHIN a domain, so the answer is three-valued rather than a Bool. A
// Bool would have to pick a side for the incomparable case, and both sides are wrong: `false` for
// "a is not before b" reads as "b is at or before a", which is a claim about an order that does not
// exist. This is the same shape as compare_content_hash returning a cross-family arm rather than
// collapsing to false.
type InstantOrder
  = InstantBefore
  | InstantSame
  | InstantAfter
  | InstantIncomparable { left: ClockDomain, right: ClockDomain }

fn compare_instants(a: ObservationInstant, b: ObservationInstant) -> InstantOrder {
  if !clock_domain_eq(a: a.domain, b: b.domain) {
    InstantIncomparable { left: a.domain, right: b.domain }
  } else if !clock_basis_eq(a: a.basis, b: b.basis) {
    InstantIncomparable { left: a.domain, right: b.domain }
  } else {
    let x = nanosecond_count(n: a.since_origin)
    let y = nanosecond_count(n: b.since_origin)
    if x < y { InstantBefore } else if x == y { InstantSame } else { InstantAfter }
  }
}
